<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
When a derivation overrides <code>configurePhase</code>, <code>buildPhase</code>,
<code>checkPhase</code>, or <code>installPhase</code>, it is expected to keep the
matching <code>runHook preX</code> and <code>runHook postX</code> calls from the
standard nixpkgs phase implementation.
</p>
<p>
Those hooks are how setup hooks, package-local customisation, and
<code>overrideAttrs</code> extensions splice extra work into a phase. Omitting one
can silently skip required build logic even when the phase body itself looks
correct.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
If you need a custom phase body, add <code>runHook preX</code> near the start of the
phase and <code>runHook postX</code> near the end, with <code>X</code> matching the phase
name. If you only need to tweak the default command, prefer the dedicated flag
attributes or smaller hook attributes over replacing the whole phase.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic — custom <code>installPhase</code> forgets the post hook:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  installPhase = ''
    runHook preInstall
    make install
  '';
}
</sample>
<p>Recommended — preserve both extension points:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  installPhase = ''
    runHook preInstall
    make install
    runHook postInstall
  '';
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/jtojnar/nixpkgs-hammering/blob/main/explanations/missing-phase-hooks.md"><code>nixpkgs-hammering</code> explanation: <code>missing-phase-hooks</code></a>
</li>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/issues/175605">Relevant nixpkgs issue</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
